terminal-router
===============

Automatic logging of every interactive terminal session, so an AI assistant
such as Claude Code can follow live work on a network device CLI in real time
without any access to the device itself.

The intended workflow: you open an SSH session to a Cisco or Huawei router in
one pane, the assistant reads ~/.claude-logs/latest in another. You type, it
reads and suggests, you decide what to run. Recording-only modes keep the log local; the AI workspace sends snapshots
to the selected provider using your CLI account.


Integrated workspace (default since 1.2.0)
-----------------------------------------

  terminal-router          router/switch shell left, AI observer right
  terminal-router claude   explicitly select Claude Code
  terminal-router codex    explicitly select Codex
  terminal-router menu     configuration and alternate launch modes

The desktop icon opens the workspace directly. Click either pane to focus it.
Only the original router pane is recorded; the observer follows its dedicated
log link across pause/resume, unaffected by other sessions. It never sends
commands to the router. Use F9/F10 to pause/resume recording even inside SSH.

In the right pane: type a question, Enter to analyse now, :auto off / :auto on
for automatic analysis, :quit to close only the observer. New output is grouped
for 2-5 seconds, with at least 10 seconds between automatic requests; model
response time is additional. Unchanged output makes no request. Context is up
to 80 lines / 16 KiB plus the previous exchange. Errors stop automatic requests.

Output is sent to your AI provider using your authenticated CLI, with normal
account usage. An already submitted request can finish after recording pauses.
Detaching tmux does not stop recording or the observer. Reattach using:
  tmux -S ~/.claude-logs/workspace-ID/tmux.sock attach

Requires Python 3 (standard library), tmux >= 3.0, and a current authenticated
Claude Code or Codex CLI. Optional models: TR_CLAUDE_MODEL / TR_CODEX_MODEL.
The observer disables shell tools and configured integrations and ignores
project configuration; Codex also uses read-only mode. Authentication is kept.
See man terminal-router for the full controls, environment and caveats.

Global recording with terminal-router install
---------------------------------------------

1. Every interactive terminal you open enters its own tmux session, via a
   marked block added to ~/.bashrc (or ~/.zshrc).
2. tmux hooks in ~/.tmux.conf intercept each new pane (session, window, split)
   and pipe its output to a logger script.
3. ~/.claude-logs/pipe-logger.sh writes a timestamped file per pane and keeps
   the symlink ~/.claude-logs/latest pointing at the newest one.
4. The assistant tails that symlink.

"terminal-router launch" is a lighter alternative to the above: it starts one
tmux session with the pipe-pane hooks set directly on that session (no -g),
so they log only its own windows/splits and vanish when the session ends.
Nothing is written to ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc or ~/.tmux.conf — every other
terminal, Terminator tabs included, is unaffected. Use it when you want
logging for a single router session without opting every terminal in.


Files
-----

  ~/.bashrc (marked block)      auto-enter tmux on interactive terminals,
                                plus the logpause / logresume functions
  ~/.tmux.conf (marked block)   hooks that start logging on each new pane
  ~/.claude-logs/               log directory, mode 0700
  ~/.claude-logs/pipe-logger.sh the logger itself
  ~/.claude-logs/tmux-<safe-label>-<timestamp>-<unique-suffix>.log
                                one file per pane, mode 0600
  ~/.claude-logs/latest         symlink to the newest pane log
  ~/.config/terminal-router/consent
                                records that this user accepted the notice
  user crontab                  nightly rotation, deletes logs over 30 days old

The marked blocks are delimited by

  # >>> claude-terminal-log >>>
  ...
  # <<< claude-terminal-log <<<

Those markers keep the name of the standalone installer script this package
replaces, so machines configured before packaging are recognised, updated and
removed correctly. Nothing else in your dotfiles is touched.


Commands
--------

  terminal-router install    set up the hooks for the current user (every terminal)
  terminal-router launch     log a single session only, no dotfile edits
  terminal-router remove     undo them (files backed up as *.terminal-router.bak)
  terminal-router status     what is active, how much has been logged
  terminal-router tail       follow the newest pane log, including latest changes
  terminal-router doctor     read-only setup, permission and live-pane checks
  terminal-router pause      suspend capture in the calling tmux pane
  terminal-router resume     resume capture in that pane, including launch mode
  terminal-router menu       configuration menu and alternate launch modes

Detaching with Ctrl+b d does not stop recording. Use terminal-router pause
before sensitive work, and resume afterwards. An already active pipe is left
unchanged. After install, shell shortcuts are also available:

Inside a logged pane:

  logpause      detach logging from this pane
  logresume     resume, into a new file

Everything is per-user and confined to $HOME. Never run these with sudo: as
root they would configure /root instead of your account.


Security
--------

The log captures everything that appears in the terminal.

Passwords typed at an interactive prompt (sudo, SSH password authentication)
are NOT captured: the tty does not echo them, so tmux pipe-pane never sees
them.

What IS captured is anything visible on screen, including:

  - passwords passed as plaintext arguments, e.g. mysql -pXXX
  - tokens in headers, e.g. curl -H "Authorization: Bearer XXX"
  - export TOKEN=... typed in the clear
  - the output of cat on a .env file or a private key

Mitigations applied automatically:

  - ~/.claude-logs is mode 0700, log files 0600, logger runs with umask 077
  - nightly rotation deletes logs older than 30 days
  - logpause / logresume kill-switch for sensitive work
  - the directory sits outside any cloud-synced folder

Remaining recommendations, not automated:

  - prefer SSH key authentication over passwords
  - run logpause before typing a secret as a command-line argument
  - immediate manual cleanup: rm ~/.claude-logs/tmux-*.log

Nothing is enabled by installing the package. Each user must opt in explicitly
and acknowledge this notice once.

install, launch and status also check whether claude or codex is on PATH and
warn (without blocking) if neither is found, since the log they set up then
has no AI assistant to read it. codex ships via nvm on some setups, so it is
only found once the shell that sources nvm is the one running the check.


Notes on Terminator
-------------------

Each Terminator tab or window opens an independent tmux session (auto-named
0, 1, 2, ...), so tabs do not mirror each other.

A Terminator split (Ctrl+Shift+O / Ctrl+Shift+E) starts bash again and thus
creates yet another tmux session. It works, but it is redundant. Prefer tmux's
own splits inside a single tab: Ctrl+b % (vertical), Ctrl+b " (horizontal),
navigate with Ctrl+b and the arrow keys. They stay in one session and each pane
is still logged separately.


Troubleshooting
---------------

The terminal does not enter tmux automatically
  It must be an interactive shell on a real tty. Scripts, cron jobs and tools
  that do not allocate a terminal are skipped on purpose, to avoid noise.

Several tabs show the same content
  Those tabs attached to an existing session instead of creating a new one.
  With the shipped block ("tmux new-session", without exec) this should not
  happen; check that the block in ~/.bashrc has not been edited by hand.

The log is empty, or latest does not exist
  Start with terminal-router doctor. It reports setup/permission errors with
  exit status 1; missing rotation or idle sessions are advisory warnings.
  An open pipe alone does not prove that data reaches disk.
  Check the hooks are loaded: tmux show-hooks -g | grep pipe-pane
  If missing, reload with tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf and start a new pane.
  After an upgrade, re-run terminal-router install to update marked blocks
  with backups. Existing panes need terminal-router pause / resume to replace
  old pipes. One-shot users can start a fresh terminal-router launch session.

Removing the package does not undo the per-user setup
  Correct: dpkg cannot safely edit every user's HOME. Run
  terminal-router remove first, or clean the marked blocks by hand.
